comments[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-26T17:18:23.106Z","harness:claude-code","Reconstruction verified end to end on 2026-07-26 against a doubly-mutated item, which is a stronger test than a single-write fixture because it proves the replay composes.\n\npm-j1fj is a closed item from the v2026.6.30 window. Today it received two independent mutations from two different lanes: a release attribution stamp and a set of evidence-derived relationship edges. Reading it at a timestamp before those writes returns release absent, updated_at 2026-06-30T05:34:40.038Z, and 2 dependencies. Reading it now returns release v2026.6.30, updated_at 2026-07-26T17:17:07.733Z, and 5 dependencies. The historical projection is not a stale cache — it is reconstructed from the stream, and it correctly excludes both of today's writes while preserving the pre-existing edges.\n\nWhat this does and does not prove, for the record this item keeps: it demonstrates single-item point-in-time projection over a multi-write day, which is the pm-hib1 capability that already shipped. It does not demonstrate workspace-level projection (pm-5gf0xf) and it does not constitute the reconstruction gate this item owns, because nothing asserts it in CI. It is a positive control worth pinning as a fixture: an item with a create-era baseline, a long quiet period, and two same-day writes from different lanes is exactly the shape a naive implementation gets wrong.\n"
  "2026-07-28T15:14:25.489Z","harness:claude-code","Reconstruction spot-check on a freshly authored stream, 2026-07-28: pm-jkbqt8 with 7 entries replays correctly at every probed version — --at 1 returns the create state with 0 dependencies and the estimate already set, --at 3 returns 10 dependencies after the wiring mutation, --at 7 matches head, and all three report reconstructed true. pm history --verify reports ok true over 7 entries with current_matches_latest true and an empty error list. One reading caveat for whoever builds this gate: the reconstructed projection reports no comments at any version, which is NOT a replay gap — pm get --json omits the comments collection entirely, exactly as it omits notes, so the same item reports 0 through get and 2 through pm comments. A gate that asserts collection equality by diffing pm get output will therefore pass vacuously on comments and notes; it has to read them through their own commands."
notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-25T20:42:51.419Z","harness:claude-code","Round-trip fidelity probed on the shipped 2026.7.25 build, so this gate's corpus can be built around cases that already hold and the one that does not. Isolated sandbox, one item taken through create, update, dependency add, note_add, files_add, comment_add, update.\n\nBackward-then-forward restore is exact. pm restore <id> 1 reduced the item to its creation state and dropped notes, files, comments, and the dependency row to zero — correct point-in-time semantics, not loss. pm restore <id> 6 then returned every one of them: title, description, the related dependency row, 1 note, 1 comment, 1 file link. Nothing in the annotation or link families needed a separate recovery path.\n\nDelete is a tombstone and is fully reversible. After pm delete <id> --message \"...\": the item file is gone, the history stream is retained, pm get reports not found, pm get <id> --at 1 still returns the full point-in-time item, and pm restore <id> 1 resurrects it with 10 fields restored and it reappears in pm list. The delete op is recorded in the stream (ops: create, comment_add, restore, delete). The read-surface half — that pm get discloses none of this — is filed separately as pm-nb4zwz.\n\nThe corpus gap this leaves is version identity. History entries carry no version field; the record keys are ts, author, author_source, agent_harness, op, patch, before_hash, after_hash, message. Version addressing for restore and --at is therefore ordinal position in the stream, while the stable identity available is after_hash. That is fine in a single lane, and I verified pm get --at 2 is stable across a branch merge, but it means this gate's compaction and merge clauses are asserting something about ordinals rather than about content-addressed states. Worth deciding explicitly in the corpus design whether a retained version's identity is its ordinal or its after_hash, because pruning and reanchoring act on the two differently."
body: ""
